///|
/// JSONTestSuite, in full: every file of `test_parsing` — 95 that must be
/// accepted, 188 that must be refused, 35 that the suite leaves to the reader —
/// and every file of `test_transform`.
///
/// The files are bytes, not text, because some of them are not valid UTF-8 and
/// that is the point of them: a parser that decodes before it looks would
/// accept a UTF-16 document that merely looks like JSON. The table is generated
/// by `scripts/gen_fixtures.py` from the corpus, which is not in this
/// repository.
///
/// ## What this reader does with the implementation-defined files
///
/// The suite files 35 cases as `i_` — either verdict is allowed. Each one falls
/// into a group, and this family decides the group once, in `Flavor`:
///
/// - **A number a double cannot hold** is accepted. The tree keeps the digits
///   it was written as, so `1e999` survives a round trip; RFC 8259 §6 sets no
///   magnitude and says an implementation has to say what it does, and this one
///   writes the document back unchanged.
/// - **Five hundred levels of nesting** are accepted, because that is what
///   `Flavor::depth` says; the file is exactly five hundred deep, and the
///   refusal begins at the five hundred and first.
/// - **A byte-order mark** is skipped when reading bytes, so the file whose
///   content is a BOM and `{}` is accepted. RFC 8259 §8.1 forbids writing one
///   and says nothing about reading one.
/// - **A lone surrogate** is refused, because `surrogates` is off: half a pair
///   is not a character, cannot be written back out, and any value put in its
///   place would be a guess. The same goes for a UTF-16 document read as UTF-8
///   and for bytes that are not UTF-8 at all — `load` decodes strictly rather
///   than guessing an encoding.
///
/// The tests below state each of those four choices on a named file, so that
/// changing one is a failing test rather than a quiet drift.
let parsing : Array[(String, String, Bool)] = [
